            {
                "content": "# SAN JOAQUIN RIVER BASIN (cont.)\n\nCHANNEL AND CONTROL.-Rough; boulder, gravel, and sand bottom; practically permanent. One channel at all stages; banks are not overflowed. EXTREMES OF DISCHARGE.-Maximum - stage during period from water-stage re- corder, 17.34 feet at 1 a. m. June 5 (discharge, 18,000 second-feet) ; minimum stage from water-stage recorder, 7.50 feet September 30 (discharge, 250 second-feet).\n\nICE.-Stage-discharge relation not affected by ice.\n\nDIVERSIONS.-Practically none.\n\nREGULATION.None.\n\nACCURACY.-Stage-discharge relation practically permanent. Rating curve well defined. Excellent gage-height record from water-stage recorder. Daily discharge ascertained by use of discharge integrator March 26 to September 2. Discharge determined by applying mean daily gage height to rating table September 3-30; estimated from incomplete day's graph March 25. Records excellent. The number of discharge measurements made during year was 87.\n\nCOOPERATION.-Gage-height record and results of discharge measurements fur- nished by Southern California Edison Co. through H. W. Dennis, construc- tion engineer.\n\nDaily discharge, in second-feet, of San Joaquin River above Big Creek, Calif., for the year ending September 30, 1922",
